## 2. Comparison of Existing Techniques

| Approach | Advantages | Disadvantages |
|----------|------------|---------------|
| Traditional Handcrafted Features (LBP, HOG, GLDS) | Simple, lightweight | Poor generalisation to unseen attacks |
| Deep Learning (CNN, ResNet, Transformer) | Learns complex patterns, high accuracy | Requires large data, computationally expensive |
| Unsupervised Learning (Autoencoder) | Detects unseen attacks without spoof samples | Limited training data, cross-sensor issues |
| Multimodal Biometrics | Improves accuracy and robustness | Adds complexity, privacy concerns |
| Lightweight Models | Suitable for edge devices | May sacrifice accuracy |

---

## 3. Research Gap Analysis

| No. | Research Gap | Evidence |
|-----|--------------|----------|
| 1 | Limited availability of puppet attack datasets | Li et al. (2024) dataset has only 70 volunteers; lacks demographic diversity; accuracy drops on angled/side pressing |
| 2 | Variations in puppet attack conditions | Models trained on straight pressing only; fail on angled/side pressing (Li et al., 2024) |
| 3 | Limited cross-dataset and real-world evaluation | Most studies use controlled benchmarks (Ametefe et al., 2024); not tested on real-world conditions |
| 4 | Balance between detection performance and computational efficiency | Accurate models are computationally expensive; lightweight models sacrifice accuracy (Yook et al., 2024; Santos et al., 2024) |

